AArch64 arm64-userword-handoff-main
(* Does the slot-claim change affect the user-word API (AllocateUserWord /
   ThisThreadUserWord / GetMinUserWord)?

   User words live in the SAME Entry cache line as localCurrentEpoch and
   threadId, and LightEpoch deliberately does not reset them on Acquire or
   Release -- the column travels with the ENTRY, so the next owner of a slot
   inherits whatever the previous owner left there. TsavoriteLog relies on
   that: each producer publishes its in-flight enqueue start address into its
   user word (BeginInflightEnqueue) and writes the InflightInactive sentinel
   back when the payload is complete (EndInflightEnqueue). SafeTailAddress is
   the min of that column, and readers use it to decide which log range is
   safe to read.

   So the handoff question is: when a thread releases its slot, is the
   InflightInactive it wrote guaranteed to be visible to the thread that claims
   the slot next?

   This is upstream's protocol: the slot is handed over by a PLAIN store to
   threadId, with the user-word write before it. A plain store carries no
   release semantics, so it can sink past the handover even though the claimer's
   CAS is a full barrier -- the message-passing pair is broken on the writer's
   side, not the reader's.

   uw=7 stands for a live in-flight address, uw=9 for InflightInactive. The
   condition is the new owner claiming the slot (its CAS reads 0) while still
   seeing the departed owner's live in-flight value.

   threadId is a 32-bit field in the C#; it is modelled 64-bit here because
   herd7's CAS takes X registers. The width is immaterial to the shape -- what
   matters is that the handover store is plain. *)
{ 0:X0=uw; 0:X1=tid; 0:X2=lce; 1:X0=tid; 1:X1=uw; uw=7; tid=3; lce=5; }
 P0            | P1               ;
 MOV X3,#9     | MOV X2,#0        ;
 STR X3,[X0]   | MOV X3,#1        ;
 STR XZR,[X2]  | CASAL X2,X3,[X0] ;
 STR XZR,[X1]  | LDR X4,[X1]      ;
exists (1:X2=0 /\ 1:X4=7)
